# NYBOSS Server Project Structure

The NYBOSS server repository contains multiple applications with different project compositions.

There is no single mandatory project structure for every application.

Existing applications must therefore be treated as structural references.

## Known Example: ManualForecast

ManualForecast contains:

ManualForecast.API
ManualForecast.Common
ManualForecast.MessageProcessing
ManualForecast.MessageProcessingHost
ManualForecast.Repository
ManualForecast.Services

Its composition is an example of a multi-project Business application.

## Known Example: TradeLifeStatus

TradeLifeStatus contains:

Aft.Services
Odin.Services
TradeLifeStatus.Common
TradeLifeStatus.Repository
TradeLifeStatusInternal.Host
TradeLifeStatusInternal.Services

This demonstrates that NYBOSS applications can have different project compositions.

## Important Rule

Do not assume that every application contains:

API
Common
Repository
Services

These are the default components for a new simple/basic/normal Business project.

Other applications may require different components.

## Existing Projects

Existing projects may use names such as:

BNPP.NYBOSS.<Project>.<Component>

This is a legacy/existing naming convention.

New projects must NOT copy that naming convention.

New projects use:

<ProjectName>.<Component>

Example:

Customer.API
Customer.Common
Customer.Repository
Customer.Services

## Application Location

Business applications are located under:

Apps/Business/

Core applications are located under:

Apps/Core/

The application location must be determined from the user's request.

If the user asks for a Business project and does not provide another location, use:

Apps/Business/

## Solution

The repository uses the existing solution:

BNPP.NYBOSS.NextGen.Server.sln

New projects belong to this solution.

Do not create a separate solution for a newly generated application.

## Phase 1 Boundary

Project structure is currently the focus.

Detailed contents inside each project will be automated in a later phase.
